prompt:
Implement a truck driving physics simulation using Three.js. Load the CesiumMilkTruck.glb model, use WASD controls for driving, and implement basic vehicle physics.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/CesiumMilkTruck.glb;
(2) Place the truck on the ground at (0,0,0) with an appropriate scale;

[Ground and Obstacles]
(3) Create a 100x100 ground plane (gray);
(4) Randomly place 5 cubic obstacles (red, dimensions 2x2x2), with y=1, distributed within the (-30,30) range;

[Driving Physics]
(5) Press W to accelerate (acceleration of 5 m/s²), and press S to brake/reverse;
(6) Press A/D to steer (maximum steering angle of 30 degrees), and return the steering to center when released;
(7) Friction: multiply the speed by a decay factor of 0.98 each frame;
(8) Maximum speed of 15 m/s;
(9) The truck's heading changes according to the steering angle;

[Wheel Rotation]
(10) Find the wheel meshes in the model by searching for names containing wheel;
(11) Rotate the wheels around the x-axis, with angular velocity proportional to the vehicle speed;

[Collision Detection]
(12) Use AABB collision detection between the truck and obstacles;
(13) On collision, stop the truck, bounce it back slightly, and increment the collision count by 1;

[Follow Camera]
(14) Make the camera follow above and behind the truck (offset 0,5,-10) and lookAt the truck;

[HUD Display]
(15) HUD in the upper-left corner:
  - Speed in km/h (id="speed")
  - Position x,z (id="position")
  - Steering angle (id="steering")
  - Number of collisions (id="collisions")

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
  - modelLoaded: boolean
  - speed: number (m/s)
  - position: {x, y, z}
  - rotation: number (truck heading in radians)
  - steeringAngle: number (current steering angle)
  - collisionCount: number
  - wheelRotation: number

Output a standalone HTML file.
